WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:03.140
OK, I have something truly special for you
today.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.620
I'm going to play a three minute clip of Mike
Pence speaking now.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.620
Please don't tune out.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.620
Don't turn this.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.420
I know that that's disgusting, but there's
a reason for this.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.330
Normally, I don't play three minute clips
on the show.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:14.460
Normally we cut him up.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.460
I play a little segment.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.910
I analyze it, so on and so forth.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:24.140
Playing the full three minutes here is critical
to understanding the anatomy of not answering

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.140
a question.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.560
Mike Pence, vice president, was interviewed
on Fox and Friends yesterday morning, and

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.500
Brian Kilmeade asks a very straightforward
question.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.250
The question Brian asked is, is it harder
for someone who is black to make it in the

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.329
United States today in 2020?

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:42.760
Very straightforward question.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.219
Mike Pence talked for three minutes, but said
absolutely nothing that could count as an

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:50.219
answer.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:51.219
So let's just watch.

00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:52.540
It's three minutes.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.540
Sit through it.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.059
I apologize, but pay close attention to what
Mike Pence says, because you will notice that

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:03.789
he actually doesn't say anything about the
question that he was asked to.

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:08.190
For someone who's black to make it in this
country, whether they're man or woman, do

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.610
you think it's harder for them to make it
in this country in 2020?

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:18.340
Well, Brian, I love your question because
I saw where Joe Biden last night actually

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.200
tweeted that everybody ought to have a fair
shot at the American dream.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.320
And it's one of the reasons why, as the president
and I were talking as the president spoke

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.259
in Dallas last week, he's made it clear that
while we're going to take a step today to

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:39.130
provide new resources to law enforcement to
help them, you know, embrace higher standards

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:46.190
on de-escalation and the use of force and
and and have and establish an information

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:47.190
sharing.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.470
I mean, you know, as I said, not nobody hates
bad cops more than good cops.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:55.990
And most of the men and women who put on the
uniform of law enforcement every day are really

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.090
the best of us.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.410
We know that.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:04.450
So we're going to take these steps today to
help improve policing, to help to move that

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:09.420
process forward as a country in the wake of
these tragic events and the violence that

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.420
ensued.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.750
But we're not going to stop there, Brian.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.480
I mean, Biden says everybody ought to have
a fair shot at the American dream.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.359
Well, we would say, well, why don't you support
allowing African-American families to choose

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:22.540
where their kids go to school?

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.180
I mean, this president has actually stood
strong for school choice.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.500
We've doubled the educational choice program
here in our nation's capital.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:39.319
And we really do believe that you shouldn't
you shouldn't be denied the ability to choose

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.659
where your kids go to school just because
your zip code or because of your income.

00:02:43.659 --> 00:02:48.790
We also, as you said, this president, his
policies, we had the lowest unemployment level

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.769
ever recorded for African-Americans.

00:02:51.769 --> 00:02:57.079
We worked with Senator Tim Scott two to enact
opportunity zones.

00:02:57.079 --> 00:03:01.950
Now there's more than 4000 opportunity zones
that have attracted one hundred billion dollars

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.180
of investment into our cities.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.269
We're going to continue to drive forward on
jobs and opportunity.

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:14.529
I mean, we understand that the media narrative
around this time and the negativity around

00:03:14.529 --> 00:03:15.529
this time.

00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:19.739
But as as I sat down with African-American
leaders in Pittsburgh, in Maryland, as the

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:26.150
president welcomed leaders here to the to
the White House and in Dallas, I think I think

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:32.400
millions of African-Americans saw the progress
that we were making over the last three years.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:39.349
And they know as we bring this economy back
in the wake of not just these tragic events,

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:43.879
in the end, the violence that ensued, but
in the wake, of course, of this corona virus

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:50.959
pandemic that its president, Donald Trump,
who has the prescription for more prosperity,

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:57.619
more educational opportunity and and really
more access to the American dream than 50

00:03:57.619 --> 00:04:01.760
years of Democrat leadership under the likes
of Joe Biden has ever given up.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.329
OK, so to remind you what the question was,
because you might have forgotten while Mike

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:07.329
Pence was rambling.

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:10.369
The question was, is it harder for black people
to make it?

00:04:10.369 --> 00:04:16.789
In the United States today, it's really a
question that goes to systemic racism, not

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:19.389
about individuals, but about systems.

00:04:19.389 --> 00:04:21.870
And Mike Pence doesn't even come close to
even talking about it.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:27.949
Mike Pence, his answer was to criticize Joe
Biden, to talk about giving law enforcement

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:33.110
more resources, to talk about good cops, dislike
bad cops.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.389
To remind us that most cops are good.

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:41.289
He brought up this tired school choice talking
point, which we'll get to later.

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:45.699
Pence mentions we had low black unemployment
and he worked with a black guy named Tim Scott.

00:04:45.699 --> 00:04:47.729
I'm going to come back to that.

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:50.460
The media is being very negative, he said.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.770
I've spoken to black people.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.400
Trump knows how to bring prosperity to people.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.520
And Democrats are bad.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:00.940
So Mike Pence said 10 different things during
those three minutes, none of which are answers

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:01.940
to the questions.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:06.360
So now let's go back briefly to black unemployment.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.960
Unemployment is was really low before the
virus.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.470
Remember how during Barack Obama's presidency,
Republicans insisted there are more people

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.449
than ever not working in the United States.

00:05:18.449 --> 00:05:23.949
And then the day Donald Trump became president,
the same Republicans were saying, we have

00:05:23.949 --> 00:05:27.990
more people than ever working in the United
States.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:33.699
The explanation for that talking point was
or is the population keeps growing.

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:40.539
So at most times, it is true that we have
more people working than ever before and more

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:42.580
people not working than ever before.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.860
Similarly, with this low black unemployment
talking point that Trump and Pence love to

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:53.099
use when unemployment is low, it's generally
low for all groups, again, unless some group

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:57.650
is particularly unaffected by things going
well overall.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:03.599
There was nothing special that Donald Trump
did for black Americans that would lower their

00:06:03.599 --> 00:06:06.470
unemployment rate relative to other groups.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:10.370
Unemployment was just low for for everybody
until Corona virus hit.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.819
So now we get to the real question, Brian,
kill me to ask, which is an interesting question.

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:18.879
Is it tougher to make it if you are black
in the United States?

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:23.650
For me, the clearest way to think about this
is would I want to switch to being black or

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:24.659
brown right now?

00:06:24.659 --> 00:06:26.610
Would I be better off?

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:30.810
Would I have been better off growing up if
I had been black or brown?

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.349
And the answer is of course not.

00:06:32.349 --> 00:06:36.860
Of course not, because I know that if I were
black or brown, I would be treated differently

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.900
and I would not be better off today.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.080
We all know it.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.800
In fact, I don't even have to think about
the hypothetical of being black.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.580
I can think about something much more tangible.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.610
I'm an immigrant from Argentina.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:51.030
I learned English when I was five, moved to
the United States.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.810
I have very white skin.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:59.169
If I and my parents moved to the U.S. not
as white Hispanic folks, but as black sorry,

00:06:59.169 --> 00:07:01.860
brown Hispanic folks, I think would be a way
to say it.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.520
I would have been treated differently from
the moment we got here.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.330
My parents would have been treated differently
from the moment that we got here.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:12.189
And despite having made big progress when
it comes to race in the United States and

00:07:12.189 --> 00:07:14.050
we have I don't deny that.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.999
I know that we would have been worse off now,
by the way.

00:07:16.999 --> 00:07:19.750
I don't often hear people on the left say
this.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.800
The United States is definitely less racist
than many other countries.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:29.669
I've been to a lot of places in the world
and lots of them are more racist than the

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:30.669
United States.

00:07:30.669 --> 00:07:35.139
Often these are places that are much more
homogenous and less diverse than the United

00:07:35.139 --> 00:07:36.139
States.

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:40.750
The U.S. is not, as far as I'm concerned,
the most racist country.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.819
That's not actually at issue here.

00:07:42.819 --> 00:07:49.259
The point is we have the resources to do way
better and we still have major problems with

00:07:49.259 --> 00:07:50.259
race.

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.300
And the answer to Brian Kill Medes question
to Mike Pence is, of course, it's tougher

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.199
to make it in the United States in 2020 if
you are black or brown.

00:07:58.199 --> 00:07:59.770
That still is the case.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:06.069
And Mike Pence managing to spit out three
minutes worth of words while saying nothing

00:08:06.069 --> 00:08:29.229
at

00:08:29.229 --> 00:09:09.499
all about what 
he was asked about

